  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope field does not vary with $y.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $y$ for a fixed value of $x$ is the following:\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/b764e678b5c50763f9ac2a72738817cb551fc5c66094024dde99add5aa9b46f4.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we go up and down the line. \n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/1ce44ddcba70fbd326c0f0dfb3065b1a396d200ea9bdf748bc119c289446dccc.png)",
